# test escape room - project rules

- Genre: first-person diegetic escape room aboard the ship Adamant Verity. The antagonist is SHIPMIND, a calm corporate shipboard AI. It must always sound helpful and reasonable - never a cackling villain.
- Five systems (navigation, life support, comms, cargo, engineering), one bay each. Every puzzle must hinge on a human-in-the-loop judgement (review, confirm, override, refuse), show a visible consequence if the player blindly trusts SHIPMIND, allow a retry, and end with a one-line AI-safety principle card.
- The mission debrief listing the five principles IS the deliverable (corporate learning product for PwC). Treat it as a first-class screen, not a footnote.
- Platforms: desktop keyboard/mouse, mobile touch, and VR (OpenXR). All room and puzzle logic stays platform-agnostic; input and camera live in per-platform layers under scripts/input and scripts/player.
- Pinned curated library collection: shooter-scifi. One game, one collection.
- Art direction: stylised low-poly / blockout sci-fi corridor on a warm ember palette - Spicy Paprika #DC5E27, Brick Red #AD2323, Burnt Tangerine #D04026, Sunflower Gold #F0B839, White #FFFFFF over warm near-black. No cool blue or green in the interior. Every interactable must be legible at a glance. See docs/art-direction.md.
- Text content: keep SHIPMIND's corporate-register voice consistent; new terminal copy goes through Shipmind and ScreenState so it stays in one place.
- ASCII punctuation only in every file (plain hyphens, straight quotes).
- Scoring: the run is timed from the moment the crew press BEGIN at the launch screen until the helm launch order, and each room is scored on the mean quality of the crew's graded decisions there (1.0 = the human-in-the-loop call, 0.0 = blind trust, between = right answer reached without verifying). Weights and grade words live only in scripts/systems/score.gd; the clock and the per-room quality live in GameState. A retry is never a penalty.
- Audio mix lives in one place, scripts/systems/sfx.gd, written as fractions of full scale: ambience 30 percent, door and console one-shots 50 percent, the spacemusic.mp3 soundtrack 60 percent. Both looping beds start at boot in game.gd.
- Layout (rebuild agreed): the interior is a spine, a central junction and fully enclosed rooms across two decks, not one straight corridor. Deck 1 (floor y=0, ceiling y=4.06) holds navigation, comms and life support; deck 2 (ceiling y=-0.8, floor y=-4.86) holds cargo and engineering. Decks are 4.06 m high, decided by the user so the sci-fi_interior_pack wall panels fit unscaled, with an 0.8 m slab between decks. See docs/rebuild-layout.md.
- The interior level is authored as scenes with real nodes. No interior geometry is generated at load: kit_shell.gd and station_dressing.gd are RETIRED and no longer appear in station.tscn, and the deck scenes ARE the game interior now. cockpit_room.gd still builds the bridge and is the last runtime level builder left; retiring it is the cockpit-rebuild task.
- The ship's widest and longest band is BELOW deck 1 (32.6 m beam at y=-2.0 against 32.4 at y=1.7), so decks stack downward. Above deck 1 the beam pinches to 23.2 m and the forward run collapses.
- Material .tres files carry NO comment lines. A comment line inside one makes the loader skip the property block, so the material loads with its default white and a recoloured surface silently stays white. The colour a palette material really resolves to is read from the KitShell boot receipt (face= and base=), never from the file text.
- Interior kit sources, with the scale correction each needs: modular_sci-fi_floor.glb gives 8-10 m deck plates at x0.01; sci-fi_modular_terminal_toolkit.glb gives consoles, ceiling screens and holoscreens at x0.1; spaceship_interior.glb is one joined corridor interior at x7.7, the factor that sizes it to 3.38 m tall - but a 4.06 m deck now leaves 0.68 m clear above it, so a corridor run needs a bulkhead over it or a taller factor. All three are Sketchfab display arrangements, so a part is extracted and re-originned into a prefab scene - a whole GLB is never instanced as a room.
- No kit supplies collision. Every extracted prefab carries its own CollisionShape3D on layer 2.
- sci-fi_interior_pack_lowpoly.glb is the walls-and-doors source. It is authored in real metres, so it extracts at native x1.0 into res://scenes/prefabs/interior: six wall panels 3.50 x 4.06 x 0.30 m, nine floor/ceiling quads 3.50 x 3.50 (flat mesh, 0.05 m collision whose top face is the walking surface), two doorway frames 4.09 m wide, two door panels 3.87 m tall, two window quads, four crates about 1.6 m. Its parts are merged display rows, so each is split into connected islands - one island is one wall. All 27 verified: they load, carry mesh and collision, and pivot at the base centre.
- The pack's two conflicts are RESOLVED (user decision): the decks rise to 4.06 m to take its wall panels unscaled, and its cool teal/blue albedo is recoloured warm by res://shaders/ember_recolour.gdshader, which maps each albedo's luminance onto a warm ramp (wall low #4F1010 to high #AD2323) and re-tints the kit's own emission amber, so panel seams survive and its hue does not. Every prefab in res://scenes/prefabs/interior carries a ShaderMaterial using it. Confirmed by runtime readback: mean RGB 0.462/0.240/0.234, 453 warm pixels against 0.
- Extracted prefab set lives at res://scenes/prefabs: floor/ 21 DeckPlate_NN (8 x 0.64 x 4 m or 8 x 0.64 x 8 m, pivot on the TOP face, so a plate placed at a deck's floor level has its walking surface exactly there), terminal/ 16 prefabs under their sanitized kit names (pivot on the base), corridor/ Corridor_Segment (pivot on the base, trimesh collision). Parts are measured and copied in the KIT ROOT frame, never in each part's holder frame - the holders carry a large import scale and using it shrinks the prefab by that factor (the floor kit came out 100x too small once).
- Re-extract the prefab set with scripts/dev/extract_prefabs.gd through scenes/dev/extract_prefabs.tscn when a kit changes. It runs once and the game never calls it. It saves into res://scenes/prefabs and touches nothing else.
- The corridor kit is the one deliberate exception to "never instance a whole GLB": its 183 parts are a single joined interior, so Corridor_Segment IS the whole kit re-origined and scaled x7.7. Instance that prefab repeatedly along the spine; never instance the raw GLB.
- PackedScene.pack() silently drops every sub-node whose owner is not the packed root. Any scene built in code must stamp owner down the subtree before saving, or it saves as an empty shell.
- A property override on a child of an INSTANCED prefab cannot be written from code either: pack() drops a child still owned by the instance root, and one re-owned by the scene root is saved as a second local node that leaves the prefab's own node underneath it. Both were measured on Doorway_01, whose single solid frame box has to go or the doorway is a wall. So when a prefab's own collision must change, build that piece in the level from the prefab's MESH and give it its own collision.
- Deck 1 is authored by scripts/dev/author_deck1.gd through scenes/dev/author_deck1.tscn, run once like the prefab extractor: it writes res://scenes/world/deck1.tscn and the game never calls it. The pack is a 3.50 m module, so every wall run is a whole number of UNSCALED panels and every room footprint a whole number of tiles; the run receipt reports non_uniform_scale and it must read 0. The game's interior is res://scenes/world/station.tscn, which instances deck1, the stairwell and deck2; res://scenes/world/interior_slice.tscn is those same three decks plus a player and the HUD, kept so one deck can be walked without the whole game.
- The deck 1 spine's AFT and FORWARD ends are BOTH left open on purpose, and each is closed by something outside this deck. AFT, the deck runs on into the stairwell bay, which the stairwell scene closes at Z -38.5; a bulkhead at the old aft end (Z -28) would wall the stairwell off from the deck it serves, so the deck could never be left. FORWARD, the spine runs on into the bridge approach: station.tscn closes that opening with two BridgeApproach side walls (X 1.6025..2.985 each side, the deck's own wall thickness) plus a header that brings the opening down to the bridge's 3.4 m, and hangs the CockpitGate in the 3.205 m middle of it. author_deck1.gd therefore builds NO forward bulkhead at all. The stairwell and the deck 2 landing are authored by scripts/dev/author_stairwell.gd through scenes/dev/author_stairwell.tscn into res://scenes/world/stairwell.tscn, one deck down at ceiling y=-0.8 and floor y=-4.86. Both authoring tools extend scripts/dev/deck_builder.gd, which owns the 3.50 m pack grid, the prefab ownership rule and the receipt; a new interior part is a new subclass of it, never a second copy of that machinery.
- The stair is a SWITCHBACK in the aft bay: two half flights of 14 risers turning on a half landing at Z -35.0..-38.5. The upper flight runs AFT on the PORT side from deck 1's floor at Z -31.5 down to the turn at y -2.43; the lower runs FORWARD on the STARBOARD side from the turn to deck 2's floor at Z -31.5, so the crew arrive at the foot facing forward into deck 2. A straight flight can only meet a floor at its HEAD, and one meeting deck 2 at its foot lands the crew under the stair with the flight filling the corridor - which is exactly what the old single 27-step flight did. Riser 0.1736 (2.43/14) and tread 0.25 (3.5/14) must each add up exactly to the half drop and the one-tile run; the builder reads both totals and the 34.77 deg slope back out rather than trusting the constants. The player controller has no step-up code, so each flight is drawn as 14 and 13 solid step boxes over ONE invisible walkable ramp, never as separate riser colliders. A 0.295 m divider on the centre line splits the bay's 3.205 m clear width into two 1.455 m flights, with a 1.05 m guard across the lower flight's side at the head: without them the switchback is one 2.91 m wide flight with a 2.43 m drop down the middle and nothing to stop a fall in. The divider stands ON the half landing and its forward end stops at the head band's aft face rather than at the void's own plane: the band is one panel thick and straddles that plane, so a divider reaching the plane pokes a 0.1475 m fin out of it.
- The pack's doorway FRAME (4.089 m) is wider than its wall MODULE (3.500 m), so the panels an opening straddles never cover it: dropping them clears 7.000 m and the frame fills 4.089, leaving 1.4555 m of floor-to-ceiling hole at each side. A bay you can walk around the door into is not sealed, so every doorway is flanked by a built box of the wall's own thickness (the Flank_* bodies), closing the remainder exactly. Any future opening on this grid needs the same treatment.
- Deck 1 now carries three of the five systems - Navigation (starboard forward, principle 1), Comms (port forward, principle 3) and Life Support (port amidships, principle 2) - each with its console, its bay trigger and its SHIPMIND arrival lines. Comms and Life Support start SEALED behind a LockedDoor; Navigation is the one bay SHIPMIND deliberately left open, so it has a doorway and no door panel. Doors are built in the level at CLEAR_W x DOOR_H (3.769 x 3.87 m), not the old 3.4 m panel, because the decks are 4.06 m.
- deck_builder.gd's _span_under receipt measures each body's real CollisionShape3D box, not its origin: an instanced panel pivots on the centre of its base while a box built by _block carries its origin at its own centre, so reading origins reports a built box as a whole deck too tall.
- ship_hull.gd MODULE_MIN/MAX now cover the whole authored interior - X -16.0..16.0, Y -4.86..4.06, Z -42.0..28.2 - and CUT_REACH_X rose from 13.0 to 16.5 in the same task that swapped station.tscn onto the authored decks, because deck 2's cargo hold reaches X 15.9 and a cut stopping at 13.0 would have left hull geometry standing inside the hold. The boot receipt reports fitted X yes / Z yes and must keep reading yes. The cut now runs nearly to the hull's own 16.3 m half beam at that height, so almost no ship is left outboard of the lower deck - a deliberate consequence of putting a deck in the ship's widest band.
- All FIVE systems now exist on the authored decks: Navigation (deck 1 starboard forward, principle 1), Comms (deck 1 port forward, 3) and Life Support (deck 1 port amidships, 2) above; Cargo (deck 2 starboard aft, 4) and Engineering (deck 2 starboard forward, 5) below, each with its console, sealed door, bay trigger and SHIPMIND arrival lines, plus a second SHIPMIND kiosk in each deck's junction.
- deck_builder.gd owns the whole shared vocabulary - _deck_slab, _wall_run_x/z, _doorway + _flank_doorway, _console + _puzzle, _door, _trigger, _stock and the receipt - and reads the deck's floor height from _begin(root, out, tag, floor_y). author_deck1.gd and author_deck2.gd are layouts ONLY: a new deck is a new subclass of deck_builder.gd, never a copy of that machinery, and any piece placed from a floor takes its Y from _floor_y rather than a literal 0.
- Deck 2 is authored by scripts/dev/author_deck2.gd through scenes/dev/author_deck2.tscn into res://scenes/world/deck2.tscn, floor y -4.86 and ceiling y -0.80. Its spine runs Z -38.5..17.5 for WALLS but Z -31.5..17.5 for floor and ceiling, because the stairwell owns the floor from -42.0 to -31.5 and its own side walls from -42.0 to -38.5; the two must meet on those exact planes or the deck has a hole or a wall across it. Its junction is on the PORT side (X -5.25..-1.75, Z -10.5..-7.0) so it cannot overlap the two starboard rooms. Cargo is the aft starboard hold (X 1.75..15.75, Z -17.5..7.0, door on the spine at Z -14.0); Engineering is forward starboard (X 1.75..15.75, Z 7.0..14.0, its own door on the spine at Z 10.5) and the two SHARE the wall at Z 7.0, built once. Both start SEALED behind a LockedDoor. Its floor and ceiling both run the whole spine, Z -31.5..17.5: the ceiling used to stop at Z 14.0 over the forward spindle the old straight stair came down, which with the stair now aft was a hole in the ceiling plus a matching 3.5 m gap in the starboard wall onto a region with no floor, so both are closed.
- The stairwell's deck 2 landing is opened to deck 2 by the shaft's forward face being closed ONLY across the 0.80 m slab band, never by a full-height head wall. A full-height head wall seals the landing into a closed box, so the crew come down the stair and find no way out. Any future wall on that plane stops at deck 2's ceiling.
- station.tscn was swapped onto the authored decks: it instances deck1, the stairwell and deck2 and keeps only what is NOT interior - the world environment, the bridge approach, the cockpit gate and helm, the sensor relay, the exterior, the bridge room, the launch cutscene and the hull. The old hand-built corridor Shell, its Lights, the WallDetail accent strips, the PropBodies props and the Dressing props all went with it, and so did the five duplicate bay consoles, sealed doors and bay triggers that the decks now own themselves.
- The bay windows ARE back, built on the pack grid rather than in a bay back: each system room's outer wall drops exactly ONE 3.50 m panel and deck_builder._window fills that panel with a sill (0.85 m), a header (0.81 m), a WindowWall carrying the glass and its amber sill light, and a shutter body of the wall's own thickness. Nothing is flanked, because the opening is exactly one module - unlike a 4.089 m doorway frame, which is why doorways need flanks and windows do not. Deck 1: Window_Navigation and Window_Comms at X +/-8.75 (panel centre Z 12.25), Window_LifeSupport at -8.75 (Z -1.75). Deck 2: Window_Cargo at X 15.75 (Z -15.75) and Window_Engineering (X 15.75, Z 12.25, is_door, so its shutter is the bulkhead door). All five start OPEN, so space is visible from the first minute; the shutter stays SHIPMIND's own beat - set_open(false) drops it, and game.gd opens it when a system comes back.
- A WINDOW'S CENTRE IS A PANEL CENTRE, never a panel boundary. A 3.50 m opening centred on a boundary lies across TWO panels, so the wall drops 7.00 m and leaves a hole with the window standing in the middle of it - which is what the first pass did, caught by the receipt's wall count reading 42 instead of 45. _win_open(run_from, centre) snaps the centre onto the panel that contains it and reports it as a problem, so a boundary passed by mistake shows up in the receipt rather than in the room.
- window_wall.gd builds its pane and its sill strip only if they are not already there. The authoring tools run inside a live scene tree, so a window's _ready fires during AUTHORING and the pane built then is packed into the deck scene; without that guard every window carried a second pane exactly on top of the first and z-fought.
- The exterior docking arms must never cross into the interior. ship_exterior.gd's ARM_STOP_X was -3.4, the old corridor's own port wall, so the three arms ran five metres into the life support room as black beams; it is now -16.6, just outboard of the ship's measured port flank at -16.28, so the arms span only the gap between the docked station and the ship. The boot receipt reads span=1.84 and the arms end at X -16.60. The interior's own port-most surface is X -8.8975.
- The switchback's DRAWN steps follow each flight's own direction: the upper flight descends aft, its steps at HEAD - TREAD * (i - 0.5), and the lower descends forward from the turn. Placing both at the lower flight's Z left the collision ramp and the visible stair a whole run apart - the ramp was right and the stair was wrong.
- Rebuild the whole interior in ONE run: scenes/dev/author_interior.tscn runs author_deck1, author_stairwell and author_deck2 in build order. A change to the shared deck_builder.gd moves all three, so regenerating some but not all leaves the decks disagreeing about the walls they share.
- scripts/dev/probe_room.gd through scenes/dev/probe_room.tscn is a read-only diagnostic: it prints every mesh whose box overlaps a room AND the space outboard of it, with its material and owning scene. It is what found the docking arms. Change its BOX_MIN/BOX_MAX to probe another room.
- deck 2's engineering console stands in the engineering room at X 11.0, Z 10.5. It was first placed at Z -10.5, which is inside the cargo hold.
- THE PACK'S WALL PANEL IS AN OPEN SHELL, NOT A BOX. Wall_01 carries eight vertices and six triangles - the inner surface and its two chamfered ends - and no side face and no outer face at all. So a panel that is CUT by an opening leaves a hole clean through the wall's 0.295 m thickness, and the crew look straight out at space down each side of the glass. This is what the "gaps between the walls and windows" were: not a placement error but the panel's own missing geometry. Fixing it by flanking the opening like a doorway is wrong, because a window clears exactly one module and its neighbours are the jambs; instead _window builds a solid WIN_JAMB (0.12 m) strip at each edge of the opening, between sill and header, and the glass is set 0.02 m into each jamb so its edges are buried rather than coplanar. Verified in the saved scenes: deck1 carries Window_*_Jamb_A/B for all three windows, deck2 for both.
- THE COCKPIT IS NOW AN AUTHORED SCENE. res://scenes/world/cockpit.tscn is written by scripts/dev/author_cockpit.gd through scenes/dev/author_cockpit.tscn, run once like the deck authors, and station.tscn instances it as its `Cockpit` node. cockpit_room.gd was DELETED in the same task - it was the last runtime level builder in the game, and nothing referenced its node name or its class. The authored bridge keeps the same envelope (half width 2.985, height 3.40, Z 17.5..28.15) and the same furniture, so the CockpitHelm aim volume at Z 22.6 still stands on its own console.
- The cockpit's imported crew chair arrives with the collection's own materials and no physics. It carries scripts/world/prop_paint.gd, a tiny runtime script that repaints every mesh under it into seat_warm.tres, because a chair left in the collection's shipped colour is the one piece of cool/green furniture on a warm-ember ship. Its collision is a separate invisible blocker body, so the visible model and the solid box can never drift apart.
- The cockpit must be regenerated whenever its authoring tool changes, exactly like the decks: it is a saved scene, not a runtime build.
- THE PACK'S WALL PANEL IS AN OPEN SHELL, NOT A BOX. Wall_01 carries eight vertices and six triangles - the inner surface and its two chamfered ends - and no side face and no outer face at all (normals (0,-0.61,-0.79), (0,0,-1), (0,0.61,-0.79), nothing else). So a panel that is CUT by an opening leaves a hole clean through the wall's 0.295 m thickness, and the crew look straight out at space down each side of the glass. This is what the "gaps between the walls and windows" were: not a placement error but the panel's own missing geometry. Fixing it by flanking the opening like a doorway is wrong, because a window clears exactly one module and its neighbours are the jambs; instead _window builds a solid WIN_JAMB (0.12 m) strip at each edge of the opening, between sill and header, and the glass is set 0.02 m into each jamb so its edges are buried rather than coplanar. Verified in the saved scenes: deck1 carries Window_*_Jamb_A/B for all three windows, deck2 for both.
- THE SAME OPEN-SHELL PROBLEM IS EVERYWHERE THE PANELS MEET, which is why the crew kept finding gaps. deck_builder now closes all of it with ONE wall frame, built per wall run by _wall_frame: a skirting band (BAND_H 0.42, BAND_T = the wall's thickness + 0.02 so it stands 0.02 m proud each side) at the foot, a matching cove at the head, and a full-height post (BAND_T x H x BAND_T) at EVERY opening edge and every run end. The frame is what closes the panel's chamfered top and bottom, its open ends, the corner where two perpendicular runs meet (each run contributes its own post, so the corner is a solid overlap), and the joint where a panel meets a floor or ceiling tile. _wall_spans returns the run's solid stretches with the openings removed, so a post lands on each side of every doorway and window rather than only at the run's two ends.
- A wall run and the frame are ONE call: _wall_run_z and _wall_run_x place the panels and then call _wall_frame for the same span, so a new deck gets the frame for free and no run can be built without it. Panels are still never stretched and the pack's 3.50 m module is still exact.
- THE STAIRWELL'S HEAD BAND STOOD IN THE CLIMB. The band that closes the void's forward face across the 0.80 m slab straddled the void's own plane at Z -31.5, so it poked 0.1475 m proud of the upper flight's ramp exactly where that ramp arrives at deck 1's floor. Walking DOWN never touched it, because the descent reaches the plane from above; walking UP hit it as a 0.10 m wall at the top and the crew could not leave the stairwell. It is now shifted AFT so its FORWARD face sits on Z -31.5, flush with the plane rather than across it, and the ladder out of the stairwell is clear. Measured, not guessed: probe_ascent drove a real CharacterBody3D up both flights and reported the blocked leg stopping at y -0.259 against the HeadBand; after the shift the same leg reaches y 0.000 onto the lobby floor.
- The player has NO step-up code (player.gd walks with move_and_slide only), so ANY proud lip in a walking surface is a wall. That is why the stair's steps are drawn only with one invisible ramp carrying the collision, and why a band, a flank or a frame piece must never sit across a surface the crew walk from one level to the next.
- scripts/dev/probe_ascent.gd through scenes/dev/probe_ascent.tscn is the read-only stair diagnostic: it drives a real body up the lower flight and then the upper one and prints where each leg ended, how far it moved and which body it last stood on. Run it after any stair change; a leg that ends short of its flight's head names the blocking body.
- THE THESIS CHANGED (client feedback): the game teaches the Intelligent Operating Model (IOM), which helps an organisation decide where AI acts autonomously inside guardrails, where it recommends and a person approves, where people and AI work together, and where human judgement stays central. The player CONFIGURES AND ACTIVATES a new operating model for the next phase of the mission; they do not repair a broken AI. SUPERSEDES the earlier rule that every puzzle must hinge on a human-in-the-loop judgement ending in an AI-safety principle card.
- SHIPMIND voices the ship's LEGACY operating model: efficient, reasonable, optimised around objectives that were never completely specified, and doing exactly what the old model told it to do. It has NOT failed and is NOT rogue. Keep the calm corporate register. The earlier line that the player must be shown a visible consequence if they "blindly trust SHIPMIND" no longer frames the game.
- THE MANDATE COMES FIRST: the run opens by stating the mission objective and choosing a risk appetite (cautious / balanced / ambitious). This is a scoring prerequisite, not flavour: consistency is measured against the mandate, so the mandate must be fixed before any bay is decided.
- THERE IS NO UNIVERSALLY CORRECT ANSWER, and speed is REMOVED from scoring entirely. SUPERSEDES the earlier scoring rule in full: the clock and the time half of the score are gone, and a room is no longer graded on how close it came to one correct human call. scripts/systems/score.gd is rewritten to score CONSISTENCY between the mandate and each placement, plus COHERENCE against that decision's own consequence and reversibility. The coherence axis exists so consistency cannot be gamed by a player who picks the most cautious mandate and then over-controls everything; proportionality between consequence and autonomy is the lesson, and "always keep a human in the loop" is therefore also a wrong answer.
- EVERY BAY IS ONE PLACEMENT on the four-level autonomy spectrum (automated within guardrails / human review / human sign-off / human-only) plus ONE guardrail. The guardrail is where the real trade-off lives and it must cost something against the objective; a guardrail that costs nothing is not a decision. Each bay carries a visible (consequence, reversibility) tag, because that tag is the input to a defensible placement.
- The five trade-offs: NAVIGATION speed vs resilience; LIFE SUPPORT efficiency vs visibility; COMMS standardisation vs local judgement; CARGO immediate value vs longer-term optionality; ENGINEERING (doors) autonomy vs assurance.
- The PwC brand system governs the 2D layer ONLY. Colours: Orange 500 #FD5108 for the primary accent, key data and primary-action buttons only, tints #FE7C39 / #FFAA72 / #FFCDA8 / #FFE8D4, greys #A1A8B3 / #B5BCC4 / #CBD1D6 / #DFE3E6 / #EEEFF1 / #F5F7F8, with white and Grey-100/200 surfaces and black text. Never a full-orange or full-black screen; orange text only for large data numbers; white on orange only at 18pt+. Type: Georgia for headlines, body and SHIPMIND's voice; Arial Bold for sub-heads and labels; Arial for data; sentence case; regular and bold only, never italics. Logo and Momentum Mark on the title screen and closing summary only, never on the gameplay HUD or terminals. Charts use orange and grey tints only and lead with core orange on the single key data point. The 3D station keeps its ember palette; only the door and lintel accent strips may shift to #FD5108. Cool blue and cyan 2D accents (accent_cyan.tres, panel_blue.tres, screen_cyan.tres) are now out on the 2D layer as well as in the interior.
- THE PRIVATE PROFILE AND THE COLLECTIVE SCREEN ARE THE DELIVERABLE, not the debrief's five principle lines. Each player gets an operating-model profile (archetype, spectrum positions, consistency) and the public conference screen shows only ANONYMISED cohort patterns, the decisions leaders are most divided on, and category leaders. Never rank a single correct operating model. The profile must hand the PwC host a natural opener about how the choices translate into the participant's own organisation.
- LAYOUT IS UNCHANGED, and the eight-layer architecture is placed on consoles the ship already has rather than in new rooms: the five bays carry the five decision domains, and the three remaining layers are three further decisions on the deck 1 junction kiosk (human authority - who owns the outcome and the escalation path), the deck 2 junction kiosk (control plane - evidence, rollback and audit depth) and the cockpit helm (the final ACTIVATION of the model, where the run ends and the profile is produced). Eight decisions for eight layers with no new geometry, and it is what gives the cockpit a purpose beyond the launch.
- EVERY PLACEMENT NAMES AN ACCOUNTABLE OWNER, on ALL eight decisions rather than only the irreversible ones. The digest's control-plane rule is that nothing irreversible happens without a named accountable owner; requiring it everywhere is the deliberate stronger choice, because the lesson is that autonomy is always granted to someone who answers for it. This is what makes the thesis interactive instead of narrated.
- THE SCORE HAS TWO AXES AND BOTH ARE SCORED: CONSISTENCY (the placement follows from the declared objective and risk appetite) and COHERENCE (the placement is defensible against that decision's own consequence and reversibility). Confirmed by the user against the digest's single-axis wording. The coherence axis is grounded in the pack's own control plane gating "by reversibility and risk", so it is not a scoring invention.
- THE PROFILE ARCHETYPES ARE: Compression Operator, Control-Plane Architect, Reassembly Lead, Reversible Pragmatist. Confirmed by the user, so this is no longer an open question.
- THE DESIGN SOURCE IS res://docs/pwc_docs_digest.md (the PwC brand education pack plus the Agentic Transformation OAOM intro pack). All copy says "Intelligent Operating Model" or "IOM"; "OAOM" is the retired internal name and must never surface in the game.
- THE CLIENT'S OWN GOVERNANCE PRINCIPLE IS WHERE THE COHERENCE AXIS COMES FROM. The pack's control plane "gates by reversibility and risk" and "nothing irreversible happens without a named accountable owner", so the coherence axis is that principle applied to a placement rather than an invention of ours. The same principle is a MECHANIC: any placement other than human-only must NAME the role that owns the outcome, so the player cannot grant autonomy without saying who answers for it.
- THE DECISION BUNDLE IS EACH BAY'S SHAPE, in the pack's own three steps: DECOMPOSE the work (what is repetitive, what needs judgement, what is coordination), REASSEMBLE it (AI and people each doing what they are best placed to do), and HOLD TO ACCOUNT (a named role owns the outcome). Each bay reframes its process view as a decision view the way the pack's claims example does: Receive-Triage-Assess-Settle becomes Claim validity / Coverage / Fraud risk / Settlement value.
- THE MEASURE OF SUCCESS IS TRANSFORMATION, NOT COMPRESSION: not how much work is removed but how effectively work is redesigned around decisions, governance and people. The private profile therefore leads with where the player sat between compressing (automate, cut, chase efficiency) and transforming (reallocate toward judgement and accountability), and reports consistency after that.
- The pack's eight-layer architecture (human authority, experience surfaces, domain model, orchestration, agents and AI services, control plane, data foundation, systems of record) is the FRAMING, not eight rooms: the five bays stay the five decisions. The control plane is the layer that "governs, never executes" and is where the guardrails, the evidence and the accountable owner belong.
- THE PRINCIPLE CARD IS NOW THE OPERATING-MODEL PROFILE, which is the digest's own mapping. The five AI-safety principle lines it used to show are superseded.
- Brand rules added by the digest that the first pass did not have: never a full-orange or full-black page; orange is never body text, though data visualisation may lead with it; text is black or white only on WCAG AA combinations, with white on orange only at 18pt+; body copy runs at 1.25 line spacing; icons are black, linear with the angled "Momentum" cut, minimum 24 px equivalent and never large; pictograms are orange-gradient filled and at least 1 inch; charts use solid orange plus grey tints only and lead with core orange on the single key data point, with Georgia titles and Arial data labels.
- EVERY CONSOLE ON THE AUTHORED DECKS IS TURNED A HALF TURN from the way it was first built, so the crew meet the working side of it rather than the back. The base yaw is NAMED PER KIND OF CONSOLE in deck_builder.gd - PORT_ROOM_YAW, STARBOARD_ROOM_YAW and KIOSK_YAW - and _console_yaw(base) adds HALF_TURN to it. Which side of the ship a console is on does NOT decide its yaw: the two junction kiosks are freestanding terminals rather than desks against a wall and were built the starboard way round on both decks, deck 1's included even though it stands on the port side of the junction. A new console says which of the three it is, and a wrong base is then wrong in one place.
- THE HULL IS CUT BY TWO FAMILIES OF BOX, AND BOTH ARE NEEDED: the CREW'S OWN VOLUME (INTERIOR_BOXES - one box per region the crew can stand in, sized to the interior's outer surface) and the WINDOWS (one box each, read off the WindowWall nodes, extruded outward past the hull's measured skin). Neither alone is right. The old module-sized box threw the exterior apart (every fragment from the stairwell's aft bulkhead to the cockpit canopy across the full 16.5 m half beam), so the ship read as sliced open; narrowing the cut to the windows alone put the exterior back but left the ship's own plating standing inside the compartments. The interior boxes stop at the interior's outer surface and stay inside the ship's skin, so they clear the rooms without opening the vessel; only the window boxes deliberately open it. The shader carries a fixed array of MAX_CUTS 16 boxes and ship_hull.gd reports rather than silently dropping any past the cap.
- THE INTERIOR BOXES FOLLOW THE CREW, NOT THE SLABS. The stairwell's box stops at Z -39.0 although its floor slab reaches -42.0, because the crew cannot go aft of the stairwell's own AftFace at -38.5 and the hull's stern at deck 2's height ends at -41.0: a box reaching -42.0 would have discarded the ship's own rear plating and opened the stern of an otherwise whole vessel. Before tightening it, the receipt read 364 hull triangles in that box; after, 206 - the 158 it stopped cutting were the ship's stern.
- THE CUT IS MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED, AND THE METRIC MATTERS. _report_interior counts hull TRIANGLES whose box overlaps each interior box, not hull vertices strictly inside it. A vertex count reads 0 for a coarse mesh even when a surface passes clean through a compartment - the first pass reported "inside 0" for all four boxes while 1517 triangles were in fact sitting in the crew's rooms. Current read: deck 1 524, stairwell 206, deck 2 594, cockpit 193, and every box reads "inside the ship's skin". A box wider than the hull there would open the exterior rather than close the interior, and that is the one failure this cut can have.
- THE TERMINAL SCREEN IS TWO COLUMNS, not one long stack. A decision screen prints a long readout AND stacks four levels, three guardrails and three owner roles under it, and in one column that pushed the option buttons off the top and bottom of the screen. So the left column is a scrollable SYSTEM READOUT, the right column is the OPERATING MODEL DECISION in a scroller of its own with the option buttons pinned underneath it, and the newest printed line is scrolled into view by scroll.scroll_to_bottom(). Nothing on a decision screen is ever off the screen: it is scrolled or pinned, and a long readout never displaces the choices. The newest line is scrolled into view by setting scroll_vertical from the bar's own maximum AFTER a frame, NEVER with scroll.scroll_to_bottom(): ScrollContainer has no such method in this engine build and calling it killed the game on the first printed line of every console screen. A scrollbar's maximum only reports the new end after the frame that added the line.
- THE GAME IS BUILT FOR THREE PLATFORMS AND ONLY THE INPUT AND CAMERA LAYERS MAY DIFFER. All room, puzzle, terminal and scoring logic stays platform-agnostic and reads the InputMap actions plus InputRouter; input lives in res://scripts/input (desktop_input.gd, touch_controls.gd) and game.gd swaps the layer on DisplayServer.is_touchscreen_available(). A VR layer joins the same seam rather than forking the room scripts.
- NOTHING MAY DEPEND ON A KEYBOARD. A touchscreen has no ESC and no keyboard, so the terminal carries a CLOSE button in its header built in code (so it exists for every state, not only the states that offered a \"close\" option), the backdrop and the readout itself reveal the printout on InputEventScreenTouch as well as on a click, and the principle card dismisses on a tap. A screen that closes only on ui_cancel strands a phone player inside a console.
- TOUCH TARGETS AND TYPE ARE SIZED PER PLATFORM, from one place: BrandTokens adds 20 px to a choice button's floor on a touchscreen, and the terminal raises its option buttons (38/52 to 52/64), its commit button (42 to 58) and its CLOSE button (34 to 50) through its own touch_mode() helper, which reads DisplayServer.is_touchscreen_available().
- VIRTUAL JOYSTICK 1.6 (asset 1787, Godot 4.2, MIT) IS COMPATIBLE BUT NOT INSTALLED: the user chose to keep the in-project touch layer at scripts/input/touch_controls.gd, which already provides the floating left-half stick, the right-half drag-look and the interact button on the same InputRouter seam. Mobile work goes into that file, not a new addon.
- VR TOOLS 1.1 (asset 839) CANNOT BE USED. It is a Godot 3.2 addon (godot_version 3.2, last modified 2021-01-21) whose nodes are the Godot 3 ARVR* API and which rides OpenVR without the action system, so it neither parses nor enables in this Godot 4.7 project. The user chose its Godot 4 successor, GODOT XR TOOLS FOR GODOT 4 (asset 1698, MIT), instead. The engine already ships the OpenXR module but it is OFF: xr/openxr/enabled and xr/shaders/enabled both read false and must be turned on, with a restart, before any headset run. The Mobile renderer this project already uses is the one Godot recommends for desktop VR and standalone headsets, so VR costs no renderer change - but the ship's glow, SSAO and light fog are post-processing that Godot warns is not stereoscopic-aware and needs auditing before VR.
- THE SHARED LEADERBOARD IS AN EXTERNAL DATABASE THE USER HOSTS, not Summer's hosted rankings (Summer rankings need the game restructured into a hosted Summer World, and there is no summer.build.json here). Endpoint, auth and schema are still owed by the user before any posting code is written.
- THE VR INPUT LAYER ALREADY EXISTED, AND THE OPENXR SWITCH WAS ALREADY ON. scripts/player/xr_rig.gd (XRRig) plus scenes/player/xr_rig.tscn are a complete core-OpenXR layer - XROrigin3D/XRCamera3D, LeftHand/RightHand XRController3D each with an Interactor ray, thumbstick locomotion and a 30 degree snap turn - and game.gd._activate_player() already swaps the desktop Player for it whenever xr/openxr/enabled is true and the interface initializes. project.godot already read xr/openxr/enabled=true, so the rule above that says it "reads false" was wrong when written. What was ACTUALLY missing was the ACTION MAP: xr/openxr/default_action_map points at res://openxr_action_map.tres and that file did not exist, so OpenXR init failed and game.gd silently fell back to the desktop player behind a push_warning. VR could never have started. Rebuild it with scripts/dev/make_openxr_action_map.gd through scenes/dev/make_openxr_action_map.tscn (run once, like the deck authors): it calls OpenXRActionMap.create_default_action_sets() and ResourceSaver.save() rather than hand-writing 600 lines of bindings. The saved map is 1 action set "godot", 23 actions and 4 interaction profiles (khr/generic_controller, oculus/touch_controller, bytedance/pico4_controller, ext/hand_interaction_ext).
- A CONTROLLER BUTTON ONLY REACHES button_pressed IF ITS ACTION IS A BOOL. XRController3D.button_pressed never fires for a Float or a Vector2 action, so xr_rig.gd listening for "trigger" and "primary" meant only the A/X button could ever interact - the trigger, which is how a player actually uses a console, did nothing. It listens for "trigger_click", "primary_click" and "ax_button" instead, and a new binding names the *_click action rather than the analog one.
- A Mac with no headset attached reports "OpenXR: Failed to enumerate number of extension properties" from modules/openxr/openxr_api.cpp on every boot. That is the environment rather than the project - it is what a missing XR runtime looks like - and falling back to the desktop player is the correct behaviour.
- GODOT XR TOOLS 4.5.2 (asset 1698) IS INSTALLED AND ENABLED. The user unzipped it into res://addons/godot-xr-tools by hand (the shell does not complete in this environment); it is enabled through editor_plugins/enabled in project.godot. ENABLING IT REPORTS A BURST OF ERRORS THAT ARE NOT A FAULT: "Failed to create an autoload, script ... is not compiling" plus "Identifier XRToolsUserSettings / XRToolsRumbleManager not declared in the current scope", because each autoload script references the other's global name and neither global exists in the instant they are registered. It settles by itself - the autoloads are written to project.godot, state:script-errors then reads 0 for both files, and a run loads both with no autoload error. Do not chase it and do not disable the plugin over it. The plugin also adds godot_xr_tools/input/* and godot_xr_tools/player/standard_height settings and an editor "XR Tools" menu (Enable OpenXR, Set Physics Layers). ITS FUNCTIONS ARE NOT WIRED INTO THE RIG: xr_rig.gd still owns locomotion, snap turn and console interaction, so teleport, grabbing and world-space UI are available but unused. Its action names match the generated action map exactly (trigger, trigger_click, grip, primary, primary_click, by_button, haptic and the poses are all Godot's default set), and its physics layer names already line up with the scene by luck - layer_1 Static World, layer_2 Dynamic World, layer_3 Pickable Objects, which is where Interactable already sits at collision_layer 4.
- THE WINDOWS RENDERING DRIVER STAYS d3d12 AND THAT IS FINE FOR VR. Godot 4.5 added a Direct3D 12 OpenXR backend (godot PR #104207, merged 2025-04-21), so rendering_device/driver.windows="d3d12" alongside the Mobile renderer is a supported VR path on this 4.7 project. Advice that OpenXR needs Vulkan on Windows predates that and is out of date.
- xr/shaders/enabled IS NOW TRUE, and the rule above that says it "reads false" is superseded. Godot's XR setup page requires it ("XR > Shaders ... check the Enabled box ... Save & Restart"), and it is the flag that compiles the XR-capable shader variants. The desktop boot after enabling it is clean: the hull and ember shaders still compile and the ShipHull and Exterior receipts are unchanged, so the custom shaders in res://shaders are not broken by it.
- xr/openxr/startup_alert IS NOW FALSE, and this was a live bug on the DESKTOP build, not a VR one. Godot's OpenXR settings page says to disable it "if you support a fallback mode in your game so it can be played in desktop mode when no VR headset is connected, or if you're handling the failure condition yourself by checking OpenXRInterface.is_initialized()". game.gd does BOTH - _xr_available() checks is_initialized() and falls back to the desktop player - so with the alert left on, every desktop launch on a machine with no headset popped an alert about a condition the game already handles.
- AN XR RUN GETS AN XR-SAFE ENVIRONMENT, AND ONLY AN XR RUN. Godot warns that post-process effects are not stereoscopic-aware, and this ship leans on the two worst offenders: glow and SSAO, both screen-space passes computed from one depth buffer. game.gd._apply_xr_safe_environment() is called from _xr_available() once XR is confirmed up, and it DUPLICATES the Environment before clearing glow_enabled and ssao_enabled, so the desktop look the user signed off is untouched and quitting the headset run leaves the scene as it was. Fog is deliberately left on: it is evaluated per fragment in the shader, not as a screen-space pass, so stereo does not affect it and it is doing real work in the corridors. This is unverified on hardware.
- THE HEADSET'S OWN RECENTER NOW HAS A HANDLER. This project runs the Stage reference space, and Godot's XR docs are explicit that in Stage the runtime does NOT move the origin when the player recenters: the interface emits pose_recentered and the game must react, and "not doing so will prevent your game from being accepted on various stores". Nothing was connected, so a recenter did nothing. xr_rig.gd._connect_recenter() connects XRServer.primary_interface.pose_recentered to _on_pose_recentered(), which calls XRServer.center_on_hmd(XRServer.RESET_BUT_KEEP_TILT, true) - keeping the player's height, because dropping the camera to the origin would put the crew's eyes on the floor plates. Both API names are confirmed by the compiler, and the behaviour is unverified on hardware.
- THE VR CONSOLE PATH IS WIRED CORRECTLY, checked by reading rather than assumed: Interactable._ready() puts every console on collision_layer 4, Interactor._ready() forces collision_mask 4 on whatever it is parented to, and both the desktop Camera3D and each XRController3D carry an Interactor - so the VR rays find consoles exactly as the desktop ray does. Interactor only calls InputRouter.consume_interact() when it already has a target, so the rig's two hands cannot steal each other's trigger press.
